      "content": "You are correct that bloom is typically associated with the separation of cocoa butter crystals, not sugar crystals. The tempering process relies on creating a symbiotic relationship between the cocoa butter crystals and impurities, like proteins, amorphous sugar, and other inclusions.\n\nWhen the tempering is off, the polar cocoa butter crystals tend to reseed and grow into nucleation sites, causing them to separate and flow to the surface of the chocolate, resulting in bloom. At a molecular level, this occurs when the mixture of \"equilibrium\" and \"non-equilibrium\" crystal structures reorganize, causing the fat molecules to reorient and align in a less stable way. The equilibrium crystals, which are the most stable form, become depleted, allowing the non-equilibrium crystals to dominate and separate.",
